# Aurora Telecom CSV

Aurora Telecom is a numbering source. It has no vendor method. The operator saves http or https links on «Загрузка нумерации». Each «Загрузить» and each «Проверить» downloads that saved list again. Do not accept a link from the load request. Do not log a URL, a query, a path, a CSV body, or a phone number.

The operator chooses the layout for each link. The stored format is the literal `short` or `full`. Do not infer the layout from the name or from the width. A file whose header or width does not match the chosen layout fails the whole set.

A short file has five columns. Column 1 is the number. Column 3 is «Покупка». A full file has six columns. Column 1 is the number. Column 4 is «Покупка». Column 2 must be exactly `СВОБОДЕН`. The header `Column 1` through `Column 5` or `Column 6` is skipped. A file without that header is data. Category, city text, and mask text are not stored.

Decode UTF-8. When the bytes are not UTF-8, decode windows-1251. A UTF-16 file fails. Accept a BOM and CRLF. Fields are separated by a comma or a semicolon. Parse quoted fields. Strip every non-digit from the number and keep only `^7\d{10}$`. Do not turn a leading 8 into 7. A number outside that shape fails the file. After trim, and after a NBSP becomes a space, a price is `^(\d+) Руб.$` stored as those digits, or exactly `ДОГОВОРНАЯ`, which leaves «Покупка» empty and keeps the number. Any other price fails the file. «Абонплата» stays empty. ABC, the local number, city, and region come from the tariff fragment. A missing tariff leaves city and region empty and the number still publishes.

Scan every downloaded file together, in list order. The first number and its price stay. A later copy is dropped and does not fail the set. A header-only file is empty and does not fail the set. An empty accepted set does not publish.

Saving a link does not download and does not change the catalog. One request stores one link, and the list holds at most 20. The address is `http` or `https`, without a login or a fragment, at most 2000 characters. `localhost`, a `.local` name, and a private, local, or service address are rejected. The ciphertext uses the same AES-256-GCM envelope as the other secrets. The settings screen may show the address. The audit may show the host and the format. The operator error and the stage log may show the host and a non-default port. They do not show the scheme, the path, or the query.

One `GET`, and only HTTP 200. Do not follow a redirect or read `Location`, including a redirect from `http` to `https` on the same host. For `https`, do not trust a custom certificate, and use the name for TLS. For `http`, do not send a TLS option. Resolve the name first. If any address is not global, do not connect. Connect only to an address from that lookup. The port and the Host header come from the saved address. A repeat resolves again. Do not use a proxy from the environment. Do not use `fetch`. Timeout 30 seconds. Read the body as a stream and stop after 20 MB decoded. Transport failure, timeout, an empty body, and HTTP 5xx retry once. HTTP 4xx, a redirect, and an oversized body do not. Discard the bytes after that file is parsed. Do not write them to disk. A socket error message is not shown: it contains the address.

The lease is claimed before the first download. The heartbeat is updated before every link. Cancel and process stop are checked there, and process stop aborts the current request. There is no second `SIGTERM` listener. A failed download, a failed decrypt, or a failed parse discards that pass and does not publish. The check downloads and does not take the database lease, write the stage log, or publish. A closed browser request aborts the check.

The provider literal is `Aurora Telecom`. One transaction replaces the free rows from the parse and clears the purchased Aurora Telecom rows. Other providers stay. The walk, the link save, and the check share one holder. The save and the check do not take the database lease. An API key cannot call these routes.
